course: biology
lesson: 322
title: Snakes to keep away from: cobras, mambas, vipers and rattlesnakes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some snakes have venom, a poison made in glands in the head and pushed into a bite through hollow or grooved teeth called fangs. Cobras, mambas, vipers and rattlesnakes are the famous ones.
explain: Most venomous snakes would much rather warn you than bite you. A cobra spreads its hood, a rattlesnake buzzes its rattle and a black mamba opens its inky black mouth, all saying «I am dangerous, go away».
explain: Films get snakes wrong all the time. Snake charmers' cobras do not dance to music, mambas do not chase horses, and sucking out venom does not help a bitten friend.
explain: Picture a walker on a heath who sees an adder sunbathing on the path: the best plan is simply to stop, step back and go round it. Snakes bite people mostly when they are trodden on, grabbed or cornered.
explain: Of about 4,000 kinds of snake, only about 200 are really dangerous to people, and many snakes help farmers by eating rats.
fact: A cobra lifts the front of its body and spreads long ribs in its neck to stretch the skin into a hood, a warning that says «I am big, leave me alone».
fact: The king cobra is the longest venomous snake, growing to about 5 metres; it eats mostly other snakes, and its science name Ophiophagus means «snake-eater».
fact: A mother king cobra builds a nest of leaves for her eggs, which almost no other snake does.
fact: Vipers have two long hollow fangs at the front that fold back against the roof of the mouth when it closes, and spare fangs wait behind them in case one breaks.
fact: A rattlesnake's rattle is a stack of loose hollow rings made of the same stuff as your fingernails, and it adds a ring each time it sheds, several times a year, so you cannot count the rings to tell its age.
fact: Black mambas are grey or olive-brown; the name comes from the inky black inside of the mouth, and their top speed is reported at only about 15 to 20 kilometres an hour.
fact: Some cobras can squirt venom from their fangs towards the eyes of an animal that threatens them; venom in the eyes must be washed out with lots of water and a doctor seen at once.
fact: The adder, with a dark zigzag down its back, is Britain's only venomous snake and also lives in Ukraine; it is shy, and deaths from adder bites in Britain are very rare.
fact: If someone is bitten, sucking, cutting, ice and tight bands do not help; keep the person calm and still, take off rings and watches, and get help at once, because hospitals have antivenom.
fact: Cobras hear sounds in the air poorly; a charmer's cobra follows the swaying pipe as if it were a threat, and India banned snake charming in 1972.
fact: Of about 4,000 kinds of snake, roughly 600 are venomous and only about 200 are really dangerous to people.
fact: Most snakes hunt rats, mice, frogs or insects and keep away from people, and snakes help farmers by eating rats that spoil grain.
exercise: How does a cobra make its hood? | It spreads long ribs in its neck to stretch the skin | It blows air into a stretchy bag on the top of its head | It grows extra skin when it is angry | It flattens its whole body like a leaf
exercise: What is a cobra's hood for? | To warn enemies to leave it alone | To catch flies like a net | To keep the rain off its eyes | To help it glide between trees
exercise: Which is the longest venomous snake? | The king cobra | The black mamba | The adder | The rattlesnake
exercise: What does the king cobra mostly eat? | Other snakes | Bananas and fruit | Only birds' eggs | Grass and leaves
exercise: What does a viper do with its long fangs when it shuts its mouth? | Folds them back against the roof of the mouth | Pulls them out and keeps them in its cheek | Sticks them out over its lips | Bites them off and grows new ones
exercise: What is a rattlesnake's rattle made of? | Loose hollow rings of the same stuff as fingernails | Tiny loose bones from the very end of its tail, joined by skin | Small stones it swallowed | Dried skin filled with seeds
exercise: Why can't you tell a rattlesnake's age by counting the rings? | It adds a ring each time it sheds, several times a year, and old ones break off | Every rattlesnake has exactly ten rings, however young or old it is | Rings are added only on its birthday | The rings fall off every winter
exercise: Why is the black mamba called «black»? | The inside of its mouth is inky black | Its skin is shiny black all over | It only comes out at night | Its venom is black
exercise: How does a black mamba usually react to people? | It slides away rather than chasing them | It chases them for kilometres across the open grass | It hunts them for food | It follows them home
exercise: Why do keepers wear goggles near some cobras? | Spitting cobras can squirt venom towards the eyes | Cobras shine bright light from their eyes | The tank glass may explode | Cobras jump out at faces
exercise: Which is Britain's only venomous snake? | The adder | The grass snake | The king cobra | The slow-worm
exercise: What should you do for someone bitten by a snake? | Keep them calm and still and call for help at once | Suck the venom out of the bite and spit it out quickly | Cut the bite and squeeze it | Tie a tight band round the arm
exercise: Why does a charmer's cobra sway along with the pipe? | It follows the moving pipe as if it were a threat | It loves the music and dances along to the tune it hears | It is copying the charmer's feet | It is trying to sleep
exercise: When did India ban snake charming? | In 1972 | In 1872 | In 2020 | It never did
exercise: Of about 4,000 kinds of snake, about how many are really dangerous to people? | About 200 | About 3,000 | All of them | About 2
exercise: How do many snakes help farmers? | They eat rats that spoil grain | They plough the soil with their tails | They scare away the rain | They pollinate the crops
test: «black mamba's black innit, it's in the name» What is the truth? | It is grey or olive-brown; the black is the inside of its mouth | Yes, black as coal from nose to tail, which helps it hide at night | No, it is bright green | No, it is see-through
test: In a cowboy film a rattlesnake buzzes before it strikes. What is the buzz really for? | Warning big animals not to step on it | Calling other snakes to help | Scaring mice out of holes | Telling the time
test: «my bro says count a rattler's rings and you get its age» What is the best answer? | No, it adds a ring each time it sheds, several times a year | Yes, it grows exactly one new ring on its birthday every year | Yes, but only for female snakes | No, rings show how many mice it ate
test: In a film the hero sucks the venom from his friend's bite. What should he do instead? | Keep the friend calm and still and get help fast for antivenom | Cut the bite open first with a clean knife, then suck the venom out | Put ice on it and wait a day | Tie a band as tight as he can
test: Count them: a museum has 4 viper skulls, each with two long front fangs. How many long fangs is that? | 8 | 4 | 6 | 16
test: Count them: 3 cobras rise up in a basket. How many hoods, legs and outer ears? | 3 hoods, no legs and no outer ears | 3 hoods, 12 legs and 6 outer ears | 6 hoods, no legs and 6 ears | 3 hoods, 6 legs and no ears
test: In The Jungle Book Rikki-Tikki-Tavi the mongoose fights two cobras that spread their hoods. What does a real spread hood mean? | «I am big, leave me alone» | «I want to be friends» | «I am about to fall asleep» | «I am hungry for fruit»
test: «adder on the path sunbathing, gonna poke it with a stick to move it» What should he do? | Leave it, step back and go round it | Poke it gently with a long stick, adders like it | Pick it up by the tail | Throw stones until it leaves
test: What would happen if all the snakes vanished from a farm? | Rats might grow in number and spoil more grain | All the rats would leave too, because rats follow the snakes | Crops would grow twice as fast | Nothing at all would change
test: Why does a spitting cobra aim at the eyes and not the legs? | Venom in the eyes hurts and blinds an attacker quickly | Legs are too far for it to see | It wants to clean the attacker's eyes | It is aiming at the ears really
test: «snake in the garden, it's probably harmless, can i pick it up» What is the best answer? | No, never touch a wild snake; leave it and tell an adult | Yes, all garden snakes are harmless, so it is fine to hold one | Yes, if you grab it by the tail | Yes, but only if it hisses
exam: Identify the function of the hood displayed by a cobra. | It constitutes a warning that deters a potential attacker | It enables the animal to glide between trees when pursued by an enemy | It serves to capture flying insects | It is employed in the incubation of eggs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the treatment of a person bitten by a venomous snake. | The person is kept calm and still, and medical assistance providing antivenom is sought without delay | An incision is made across the wound, the venom is extracted by suction and a tight ligature is applied above the bite | The venom is extracted by suction from the wound | A tight ligature is applied above the bite
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of a cobra before a snake charmer? | It follows the movement of the pipe as though it were a threat | It responds with evident pleasure to the music and moves in time to it | It performs a dance learnt from its keeper | It is asleep and sways involuntarily
exam: Ascertain the approximate proportion of snake species that are seriously dangerous to human beings. | About two hundred out of some four thousand species | About three thousand out of some four thousand species | Every species without exception | No species whatsoever
